# Warp session (cloud and local)

Plugin hooks do not run on cloud runners. Do not wait for `sessionStart`, `stop`, `subagentStart`, `subagentStop`, or `beforeMCPExecution`. The scripts below are the path. A local IDE hook only repeats the same script.

`<plugin>` is the loaded plugin, often `.cursor/plugins/warp` after `/warp-init`. In this repo it is the repo root.

## Before dispatch

Run this and follow it. Do not dispatch from the hint.

```bash
python3 <plugin>/scripts/resume_hint.py --root .
```

If it says there is no beam, stop and tell the user to run `/warp-ingest`. If the beam is paused, do not dispatch. Read `.warp/BOARD.md` and run `/warp-status` before claiming tickets.

## Who starts and who waits

One agent is long-lived: the parent, the session that ran `/warp-start` or `/warp-resume`. It is the only agent that starts another agent, and the only agent that waits. Every other Warp agent does one step and returns one line. `docs/LIFECYCLE.md` is the rule.

If you are a Shuttle or the listener: do not start an agent, subscribe, set a timer, loop, sleep, or wait. Run the reap check from your prompt first, and stop on `reap: exit`.

```bash
python3 <plugin>/scripts/agents.py reap --beam .warp/beam.json --id <agent> --ticket <id>
```

If you are the parent: start a Shuttle only from the prompt `checkout.py launch` prints, start the listener only on `listener: poll`, and wait only with this command at the end of each pass.

```bash
python3 <plugin>/scripts/orchestrator.py parent-exit --beam .warp/beam.json --wait --session <session>
```

`/warp-pause` and `/warp-stop` end every agent in `.warp/agents.json`. After either one, start nothing.

## When a Warp turn ends

`/warp-stop` and `/warp-pause` already append a `session-stop` journal line. At the end of a `/warp` tick, or when this Warp session is ending, run:

```bash
python3 <plugin>/scripts/session_note.py --type session-stop --root .
```

A second call is skipped while that line is still the last journal event.

## When a Shuttle finishes

You were started with `IMPLEMENT <id>`, or a Shuttle you spawned returned. Record it, then reconcile that ticket from the beam on the next tick. The journal line is a record. The beam is the source of truth.

```bash
python3 <plugin>/scripts/session_note.py --type subagent-stop --root .
```

## MCP tools

Call only Warp's allowlisted tools: Slack, Teams, and Jira from `scripts/mcp_tools.py`, GitHub `add_issue_comment` when git comments are on, and `notifyAllow` pairs. Before any other MCP tool, run:

```bash
python3 <plugin>/scripts/mcp_allow.py --server SERVER --tool TOOL --root .
```

If it prints `ask`, do not call the tool. Do not widen the list. Do not pass a server wildcard. `--allow-server-tools` stays off unless the user set it. A missing hook is not permission to run every MCP tool or every shell command. This check does not approve shell commands. Local IDE prompts still follow `.cursor/permissions.json`.

## Ticket subagents

Start one subagent per ticket, in parallel up to `maxAgents`. Only the parent does this. Pass each subagent only the lines below the `prompt:` mark that `checkout.py launch` prints. Subagent frontmatter has no isolation field. The fields are name, description, model, readonly, and is_background. Isolation is requested in the prompt. `subagentVm` defaults to false. `maxAgents` is the one cap on agents that run at once. `memoryCheck` defaults to false.

`runner` is the main driver. `runner: local`, and `auto` that resolves to local, forces `subagentVm` false and `launch` to `worktree` even when the file says otherwise. That start skips the cloud-environment check and logs one note. Start, `/warp-version`, and status print the effective values after the override.

When `subagentVm` is true on a cloud runner, the prompt must say: "Run in your own cloud environment on a dedicated VM with its own clone and branch, not a git worktree on this machine." The subagent fetches the latest main, clones it, and creates `warp/<id>-<jira>` itself. Its first step is `hostname` and `free -g`. It reports that output with its branch name and working directory. It writes `.warp/tickets/<id>/` on its own branch and pushes. That write records its cloud agent id, so pause and stop can cancel it by id. The parent patches the beam from those folders. The parent's disk is not shared. Its reap check passes `--remote` and reads the beam from origin's base branch. The subagent never merges, never touches the parent checkout, and never calls Jira or Slack. It returns one line.

After each subagent, run `checkout.py verify`. Compare the hostname it reports with this machine's `hostname`. A different hostname is a separate VM. The same hostname means the ticket fell back to a worktree on this machine. Record that in the ticket log, run that ticket under the shared-machine rules, and post one warning per run.

When `subagentVm` is false, or on that fallback, use a git worktree under `worktreeRoot`. Start one subagent per ticket in its own worktree, in parallel up to `maxAgents`. `memoryCheck: true` lets `free -g` lower that cap when memory is tight. At least one may run. With `memoryCheck` false, only `maxAgents` limits how many run at once. The subagent writes `.warp/tickets/<id>/state.json` and `log.jsonl` into the parent checkout by absolute path.

A failed subagent result raises `worker-died`. The heartbeat watchdog still covers a parent that dies mid-turn. `/warp-resume` reuses surviving worktrees and branches.

On a cloud runner, effective `subagentVm: true` warns, and `--force` allows the start, when `.cursor/environment.json` and `cloudSnapshot` are both missing. A local runner skips that check. Cloud subagents use the MCP servers configured at cursor.com/agents, not the local session's.

In the Agents Window, `/in-cloud` runs one task as a cloud subagent on its own VM and branch. It is a manual fallback, one ticket per invocation.

Optional `launch: agent` prints an IMPLEMENT prompt for one new Agent. This plugin does not call a Cloud Agents API.
